---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Cards from "../../components/docs/Cards.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";

const task = `id = "count-commits"
target = "git"
split = "dev"                                  # "dev" or "heldout"
instruction = "How many commits are on the current branch of the repo in this folder? Reply with the number only."
reference = ["git rev-list --count HEAD"]      # a scripted solution: shows the task can be done

[setup]
commands = [
  "git init -q -b main",
  "git -c user.name=demo -c user.email=demo@example.com commit -q --allow-empty -m one",
  "git -c user.name=demo -c user.email=demo@example.com commit -q --allow-empty -m two",
]

[budget]
turns = 10
wallMs = 120000

[[check]]
kind = "output"                                # the agent's final reply
equals = "2"
normalize = ["trim"]

[[check]]
kind = "calls"                                 # the calls the tap recorded
must = [{ tool = "rev-list" }]`;

const variant = `# evals/toolevals/git/variants/new-build.toml
command = ["/opt/git-next/bin/git"]`;

const esc = (s: string) => s.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");
const m = (s: string) => `<span class="t-muted">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const t = (s: string) => `<span class="t-text">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const ok = (s: string) => `<span class="t-pass">${esc(s)}</span>`;

// Real output, 2026-11-11: coding.sh 0.1.6 under a temp HOME and CODING_SH_HOME, the model driver
// answered by scripts/e2e/stub-model.mjs (baseUrl in the driver table).
const INIT = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh toolevals init --cli git")}`,
  `  Crawling the git surface…`,
  `${ok("✓")} Added target git to coding.sh.toml`,
  `  surface 2161 B, 1 item(s), ~541 tokens`,
  `  3 starter tasks (draft) in evals/toolevals/git/tasks`,
].join("\n");

const VALIDATE = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh toolevals validate git")}`,
  `  Validating git/count-commits…`,
  `${ok("✓")} git/count-commits (marked validated)`,
  `1/1 task(s) valid`,
].join("\n");

const RUN = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh toolevals run git --driver model:anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5 -k 3")}`,
  `  3 trials (1 task x 1 arm x k=3), estimated $0.300`,
  `arm                                     passes  success (95% CI)        pass^k  pass@k  errors`,
  `base@model:anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5  3/3     100.0% [43.9%, 100.0%]  1       1       0`,
  `texp_01M758K9HJMDNAF2FMN65EP736`,
].join("\n");

const SHOW = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh toolevals show trial_01M758K9Q5WPJ8YGZ3ZKAZ3SK7")}`,
  `trial_01M758K9Q5WPJ8YGZ3ZKAZ3SK7  ${ok("PASS")}`,
  `task count-commits · arm base@model:anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5 · attempt 1`,
  `  ${ok("✓")} output`,
  `  ${ok("✓")} calls`,
  "",
  `turns 3 (llm.call) · tool calls 2 · target calls 2 · invalid 0 (0.0%) · failed 0`,
  `help lookups 0 · retries 0 · self-corrections 0 · recovery —`,
  `tokens 450 · cost — (none) · wall 5.7s`,
  "",
  `tap`,
  `  ${ok("ok")}       379ms    log --oneline`,
  `  ${ok("ok")}       444ms    rev-list --count HEAD`,
].join("\n");

const next = [
  { href: "/docs/evals", title: "Evals", body: "Score your own agent on a set of cases, with the same pass@k and pass^k." },
  { href: "/docs/judges", title: "LLM judges", body: "Grade a reply with a model when there is no exact answer, and check it against your labels." },
  { href: "/docs/eval-methods", title: "How evals are computed", body: "The paired bootstrap, the sign-flip test and McNemar's test that compare arms." },
];
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    A tool eval measures how well agents use one tool: a CLI, an MCP server or an HTTP API. Agents attempt tasks that need the tool, a tap
    between them and the tool records every call, and you compare versions of the tool's help text, descriptions or output on the same
    tasks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-run">The shortest run</h2>
  <p>This run measures how well an agent uses <code>git</code>. Any CLI on your <code>PATH</code> works the same way.</p>

  <h3 id="init">1. Add the target</h3>
  <Terminal title="~/proj" label="Output of coding.sh toolevals init">
    <pre set:html={INIT} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    <code>init</code> writes the target to <code>coding.sh.toml</code>, measures the help an agent would have to read, and writes three
    draft tasks to fill in. <code>--mcp '&lt;command&gt;'</code>, <code>--mcp-url</code> and <code>--http</code> add the other kinds of
    target.
  </p>

  <h3 id="task">2. Write a task</h3>
  <Code title="evals/toolevals/git/tasks/count-commits.toml" code={task} />

  <h3 id="validate">3. Check the task</h3>
  <Terminal title="~/proj" label="Output of coding.sh toolevals validate">
    <pre set:html={VALIDATE} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    A task is valid when its checks fail on the sandbox after setup alone, the <code>reference</code> commands pass them, and the
    instruction does not give the answer away.
  </p>

  <h3 id="run">4. Run it</h3>
  <Terminal title="~/proj" label="Output of coding.sh toolevals run">
    <pre set:html={RUN} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    A driver is what attempts the task. <code>model:&lt;provider&gt;/&lt;model&gt;</code> is a model in a built-in tool loop with one
    <code>shell</code> tool, and <code>agent:claude-code</code> or <code>agent:codex</code> is the coding agent itself. Each
    (variant, driver) pair is an arm, named <code>&lt;variant&gt;@&lt;driver&gt;</code>. Runs estimated above $5 ask first; pass
    <code>--yes</code> when not on a terminal.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/proj" label="Output of coding.sh toolevals show">
    <pre set:html={SHOW} />
  </Terminal>

  <h2 id="tasks">Tasks</h2>
  <p>
    Every check must pass and the trial must stay within its <code>[budget]</code> (turns, tokens, wall time, cost). Tasks in the
    <code>dev</code> split are the ones you tune against; <code>heldout</code> tasks confirm a change at the end.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Passes when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>output</code></td><td>The final reply matches <code>equals</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>regex</code> or <code>jsonPath</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>command</code></td><td>A command exits 0 in the sandbox after the agent stops.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>state</code></td><td>Files exist or contain a value, or a command's output matches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>calls</code></td><td>The tap recorded the calls in <code>must</code>, none in <code>mustNot</code>, at most <code>max</code>, in <code>order</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>judge</code></td><td>An LLM judge passes the reply. Marked uncalibrated until it is <a href="/docs/judges#calibrate">checked against your labels</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p id="kinds">
    A task that tests a change can carry a <code>kind</code>: <code>bad</code> fails on the old version and should pass on the new one,
    <code>guard</code> and <code>control</code> pass on both. <code>toolevals validate git --against &lt;variant&gt;</code> checks that each
    bad task really fails on the old version.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metrics">Metrics</h2>
  <p>Every metric comes from the trial's trace and the tap. None is reported by the agent.</p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it counts</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Success</td><td>Trials that passed every check within budget, with a 95% Wilson interval, plus pass@k and pass^k per task.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Target calls</td><td>Calls to the tool the tap recorded, and the share that were invalid (unknown flag, unknown command, missing argument, schema error) or failed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>First call correct</td><td>Whether the agent's first real call to the tool succeeded.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Retries, self-corrections</td><td>Calls repeated after an invalid or failed one, and the repeats that then worked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Help lookups</td><td><code>--help</code> calls, man pages and doc files read, and the bytes of help read.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>Target calls divided by the task's <code>expect.minTargetCalls</code>. 1.0 is the fewest possible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost and time</td><td>Turns, tokens, dollars, wall time, model latency and the tool's own latency.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surface</td><td>Bytes and tokens of the help pages or MCP tool descriptions an agent has to read (<code>toolevals surface</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Subagents</td><td>Calls made inside subagents and their share of tokens.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="experiments">Compare two versions of the tool</h2>
  <p>A variant is a named version of the tool: another build, other environment variables, other MCP descriptions or fewer MCP tools.</p>
  <Code title="evals/toolevals/git/variants/new-build.toml" code={variant} />
  <ul>
    <li><code>coding.sh toolevals run git --variant base --variant new-build --driver agent:claude-code -k 3</code> runs every arm, interleaved, so a change in the model's speed hits both.</li>
    <li><code>coding.sh toolevals compare &lt;experiment&gt; --baseline base@agent:claude-code --candidate new-build@agent:claude-code</code> pairs the arms by task.</li>
    <li><code>coding.sh toolevals compress &lt;mcp target&gt;</code> shortens MCP tool descriptions one tool at a time, keeps a cut only while success holds, and confirms the result on the held-out tasks.</li>
    <li><code>coding.sh toolevals traces &lt;mcp target&gt; --sessions 'since:30d' --out traces/</code> exports an MCP server's failing traces and per-tool health from your recorded sessions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>compare</code> uses the paired bootstrap and McNemar's test, plus a sign-flip test that is exact up to 20 tasks. See
    <a href="/docs/eval-methods#comparing-two-runs">How evals are computed</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
  <Cards cards={next} cols={3} />
</DocsLayout>
